.TH MSTREMOTE "1" "September 2026" "mstflint" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mstremote \- register devices served by a remote mstserver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mstremote add
\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIpassphrase\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B mstremote del
\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mstremote
asks an
.BR mstserver (1)
which devices it serves and registers them locally, so the ordinary mstflint
tools can address them and
.BR mstdevices_info (1)
lists them under "Remote devices".
.PP
A registered device is an empty marker file in \fI/dev/mstflint\fR whose name is the
device name, \fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR,\fIdevice\fR, with '@' standing for '/'. The
file holds no data and is never opened; it exists so the device can be
discovered. Naming a remote device works whether or not it is registered:
.IP
mstflint \-d myhost:23108,0000:43:00.0 q
.PP
.B add
authenticates first. When
.B \-s
is not given the passphrase is read from the terminal without echoing. The
resulting session token is written to
\fI/var/run/mstflint/.token.<host>_<port>\fR, where the tools pick it up. Registering
and unregistering need root; using the devices afterwards does not.
.SS Server identity
The connection is TLS, and the server proves who it is with a key it generates
once and keeps. The first time this host talks to a given server, its key is
unknown, so
.B mstremote
prints the key's fingerprint and asks whether to continue, exactly as
.BR ssh (1)
does for an unknown host:
.IP
The authenticity of MST server 'myhost:23108' can't be established.
.br
Server key fingerprint is SHA256:2YMOeTLaODASHO/h44tV8KPGg4xG1X+TOQIZVjC2ghA.
.br
Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)?
.PP
Compare that fingerprint with the one
.B mstserver start
printed on the server before answering; the question is only worth asking if the
answer is checked. Answering yes records the key in
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_known_hosts\fR and it is not asked again.
.PP
If the key later changes, every command against that server fails and says so.
No option overrides this: a changed key is what an interposed server looks like,
and the only safe resolution is for an operator to establish out of band that
the server really was reinstalled, then remove its line from the file. Nothing
in this tool rewrites an existing entry.
.PP
When there is no terminal to ask on, an unknown server is currently accepted
with a warning rather than refused. Automated callers should pass
.B \-y
so that behaviour does not have to be relied upon.
.PP
By default one entry is registered per device. A server reports each device
twice, under its bus address and its sysfs configuration-space path; both refer
to the same hardware, so only the bus address is kept.
.PP
The markers go in \fI/dev/mstflint\fR rather than in MFT's \fI/dev/mst\fR,
even though the naming is MFT's. MFT clears \fI/dev/mst\fR on
.BR "mst stop" ,
and its
.B "mst remote del"
removes the same
\fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR,* pattern written here, so sharing the directory would
let an unrelated MFT command silently unregister these devices. Devices
registered with MFT's
.B "mst remote add"
are correspondingly not visible here.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-passphrase\fR \fIpassphrase\fR
Passphrase of the remote server. Prompted for when omitted, which is preferred:
an argument is visible in \fBps\fR(1) and recorded in the shell history.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Trust an unrecognized server without asking. Intended for automated callers,
which have no terminal to answer on. It has no effect on a server whose
recorded key has changed, which is refused either way.
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Register every name the server reports, including the configuration-space alias
of each device.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show usage and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MSTFLINT_REMOTE_DEV_DIR
Directory holding the marker files, instead of \fI/dev/mstflint\fR. Useful for
registering without root.
.TP
.B MSTFLINT_REMOTE_TOKEN
Session token to use, instead of reading one from \fI/var/run/mstflint\fR.
.TP
.B MST_REMOTE_AUTO_TRUST
Set to a value other than 0 to behave as if
.B \-y
had been passed, for environments that cannot change every call site.
.TP
.B MST_KNOWN_HOSTS
File recording the trusted server keys, instead of
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_known_hosts\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
# mstremote add myhost:23108
.br
The authenticity of MST server 'myhost:23108' can't be established.
.br
Server key fingerprint is SHA256:2YMOeTLaODASHO/h44tV8KPGg4xG1X+TOQIZVjC2ghA.
.br
Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)? yes
.br
Passphrase for myhost:23108:
.br
\-I\- 8 remote device(s) registered from myhost:23108
.PP
.IP
$ mstdevices_info
.br
$ mstflint \-d myhost:23108,0000:43:00.0 q
.PP
.IP
# mstremote del myhost:23108
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI/dev/mstflint/<host>:<port>,<device>\fR
Marker file for a registered remote device.
.TP
\fI/var/run/mstflint/.token.<host>_<port>\fR
Session token for that server.
.TP
\fI/var/lib/mstflint/mst_known_hosts\fR
Server keys this host has been told to trust, one
"\fIhost\fR:\fIport\fR \fIfingerprint\fR" line each.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B mstserver(1), mstdevices_info(1), mstflint(1)
